**Step 1:**
 Sodium metal ([Na:9]) is introduced to liquid ammonia ([NH3]). Sodium metal readily donates its valence electron to the solvent ammonia molecules. This electron donation results in the formation of solvated electrons within the ammonia solvent. These solvated electrons are highly reducing agents and are responsible for subsequent reactions involving electron transfer processes.

**Step 2:**
 The solvated electrons generated in the previous step act as powerful reducing agents. Although not explicitly shown in the reactants SMILES for this step, phenanthrene is implicitly present as the substrate undergoing reduction alongside the sodium metal and liquid ammonia. A solvated electron will attack one of the aromatic rings of phenanthrene in an single electron transfer (SET) process. This electron addition disrupts the aromaticity of the ring, leading to the formation of a radical anion intermediate where one electron pair resides in a non-bbonding orbital localized across the ring system.

**Step 3:**
 The radical anion intermediate formed in the previous step is highly reactive due to its negative charge and radical character. In the presence of ethanol solvent molecules ([ ethanol]), the radical anion will abstract a proton from ethanol. This protonation event neutralizes one of the negative charges on the ring system, converting the radical anion into a neutral radical intermediate.

**Step 4:**
 The neutral radical intermediate formed in the previous step still possesses one unpaired electron ( radical character). Another solvated electron from the sodium/ammonia mixture will be transferred to this radical intermediate in another single electron transfer (SET) process. This electron addition restores the negative charge to the ring system, forming a dianion intermediate where two electrons are now present in non-bbonding orbitals across the ring system.

